# This is a plain example/template, NOT a real vault file - it is meant
# to be read, not decrypted (there's nothing encrypted about it). Create
# the real group_vars/all/vault.yml alongside it by running, once per
# environment, from this ansible/ directory:
#
#   ansible-vault encrypt_string --ask-vault-pass \
#     --name 'secretweb_ca_passphrase_prod' \
#     "$(python3 -c 'import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))')"
#
#   ansible-vault encrypt_string --ask-vault-pass \
#     --name 'secretweb_ca_passphrase_test' \
#     "$(python3 -c 'import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))')"
#
# Pick a vault password when prompted (you'll need it again on every
# future run that touches PKI - see roles/secretweb_pki/tasks/main.yml).
# Use a DIFFERENT random passphrase for each environment (two separate
# `python3 -c ...` invocations above, not the same value pasted twice) -
# they encrypt two entirely separate CAs, so there's no reason for a
# prod passphrase leak to also expose the test CA's key or vice versa.
#
# Paste each command's full output (the "secretweb_ca_passphrase_prod: !vault
# |\n          $ANSIBLE_VAULT;1.1;AES256\n..." block) into
# group_vars/all/vault.yml - that whole file is then safe to commit; only
# the vault password itself (which you typed above, and which lives
# nowhere in this repo) can decrypt it.
#
# Real group_vars/all/vault.yml ends up looking like this (illustrative
# values only - do not use these):
#
# secretweb_ca_passphrase_prod: !vault |
#           $ANSIBLE_VAULT;1.1;AES256
#           66386134653765386231653966386231653966386231653966386231653966
#           38623165396638623165396638623165396638623165396638623165396638
#           ...
# secretweb_ca_passphrase_test: !vault |
#           $ANSIBLE_VAULT;1.1;AES256
#           37653834663738653834663738653834663738653834663738653834663738
#           ...
